# IMO 2014 A4 — Solution

**Problem.** Let $b,c\in\mathbb Z$ with $|b|>1$, $c\ne 0$. Find all $f:\mathbb Z\to\mathbb Z$ such that for all $x,y\in\mathbb Z$,
$$f(y+f(x))-f(y)=f(bx)-f(x)+c. \tag{P(x,y)}$$

---

## Step 1. The difference $f(y+t)-f(y)$ is independent of $y$ for every $t\in\operatorname{Im}(f)$.

The right-hand side of (P) depends only on $x$. Hence for fixed $x$, the quantity $f(y+f(x))-f(y)$ is constant in $y$. Define
$$\phi(t):=f(y+t)-f(y)\qquad(t\in\operatorname{Im}(f)),$$
which is well defined (independent of $y$) by the observation above. Then (P) reads
$$\phi(f(x))=f(bx)-f(x)+c. \tag{1}$$

## Step 2. Extend $\phi$ to a homomorphism on the subgroup generated by the image.

Let $G=\langle\operatorname{Im}(f)\rangle\le\mathbb Z$. If $\operatorname{Im}(f)=\{0\}$ then $f\equiv0$ and (P) gives $c=0$, contradiction. So $G=d\mathbb Z$ for some $d\ge1$.

For $t_1,t_2\in\operatorname{Im}(f)$:
$$f(y+t_1+t_2)=f(y+t_1)+\phi(t_2)=f(y)+\phi(t_1)+\phi(t_2),$$
so $f(y+(t_1+t_2))-f(y)=\phi(t_1)+\phi(t_2)$, independent of $y$. Likewise $f(y-t)-f(y)=-\phi(t)$ for $t\in\operatorname{Im}(f)$. By induction, for **every** $t\in G$ the difference $f(y+t)-f(y)$ is independent of $y$. Define
$$\tilde\phi(t):=f(y+t)-f(y),\qquad t\in G.$$
One checks $\tilde\phi(t_1+t_2)=\tilde\phi(t_1)+\tilde\phi(t_2)$, so $\tilde\phi:G\to\mathbb Z$ is a group homomorphism. Writing $\alpha:=\tilde\phi(d)$, we have
$$\tilde\phi(t)=\frac{\alpha}{d}\,t\qquad(t\in d\mathbb Z). \tag{2}$$

## Step 3. $f$ is affine on each coset of $d\mathbb Z$.

For $x\equiv r\pmod d$ (with $r\in\{0,\dots,d-1\}$), write $x=qd+r$. Since $f(x)=f(r+qd)=f(r)+\tilde\phi(qd)=f(r)+q\alpha$, we get
$$f(x)=\frac{\alpha}{d}\,x+\delta_r,\qquad\text{where }\delta_r:=f(r)-(b-1)r\text{ will be chosen so that }\delta_r:=f(r)-\tfrac{\alpha r}{d}. \tag{3}$$
(Concretely $\delta_r=f(r)-\frac{\alpha r}{d}$; then $f(x)=\frac{\alpha x}{d}+\delta_r$ for $x\equiv r\pmod d$.)

## Step 4. Plug into the equation.

Since $f(x)\in\operatorname{Im}(f)\subseteq G$, equation (1) and (2) give
$$\frac{\alpha\,f(x)}{d}=f(bx)-f(x)+c. \tag{4}$$
Substituting (3) on both sides, with $r_x=x\bmod d$ and $r_{bx}=bx\bmod d$:
$$\frac{\alpha}{d}\Big(\frac{\alpha x}{d}+\delta_{r_x}\Big)=\frac{\alpha bx}{d}+\delta_{r_{bx}}-\frac{\alpha x}{d}-\delta_{r_x}+c,$$
i.e.
$$\underbrace{\frac{\alpha\big(\alpha-(b-1)d\big)}{d^2}}_{=:A}\,x=\delta_{r_{bx}}-b\,\delta_{r_x}+c. \tag{5}$$
The right side depends only on the residue $r_x$, while the left side is $A\,x$. Fixing $r$ and letting $x=qd+r$ vary, the left side changes by $Ad$ per unit increase of $q$ but the right side is constant; hence $Ad=0$. Since $d\ge1$:
$$\alpha=0\quad\text{or}\quad \alpha=(b-1)d. \tag{6}$$

## Step 5. Rule out $\alpha=0$.

If $\alpha=0$ then $\tilde\phi\equiv0$, so $f$ is constant on each coset: $f(x)=\gamma_{r_x}$. Equation (4) gives $0=\gamma_{r_{bx}}-\gamma_{r_x}+c$, i.e.
$$\gamma_{br\bmod d}=\gamma_r-c\qquad\forall\,r. \tag{7}$$
Let $\sigma:r\mapsto br\bmod d$ on the finite set $\mathbb Z/d\mathbb Z$. Iterating (7): $\gamma_{\sigma^k(r)}=\gamma_r-kc$. Since $\sigma$ acts on a finite set, $\sigma^k(r)=r$ for some $k\ge1$, giving $kc=0$, hence $c=0$ — contradiction. So $\alpha\ne0$.

## Step 6. The case $\alpha=(b-1)d$.

Now (5) reduces to
$$\delta_{br\bmod d}=b\,\delta_r-c\qquad\forall\,r\in\{0,\dots,d-1\}. \tag{8}$$
Iterating: $\delta_{\sigma^k(r)}=b^k\delta_r-c\,\dfrac{b^k-1}{b-1}$. Again $\sigma^k(r)=r$ for some $k\ge1$ (finiteness), so
$$\delta_r=b^k\delta_r-c\,\frac{b^k-1}{b-1}.$$
Since $|b|>1$, we have $|b^k|>1$, so $b^k\ne1$, and we may solve:
$$\delta_r=\frac{c}{b-1}\qquad\forall\,r. \tag{9}$$
(One obtains this for the periodic point $s=\sigma^i(r)$ first, then propagates backward using $\delta_{r'}=\frac{\delta_{\sigma(r')}+c}{b}=\frac{c/(b-1)+c}{b}=\frac{c}{b-1}$.)

## Step 7. Determine $f$.

From (3) and (9):
$$f(x)=\frac{(b-1)d}{d}\,x+\frac{c}{b-1}=(b-1)x+\frac{c}{b-1}.$$
For $f$ to be integer-valued we need $\frac{c}{b-1}\in\mathbb Z$, i.e. $(b-1)\mid c$.

## Step 8. Verification.

Let $f(x)=(b-1)x+\dfrac{c}{b-1}$ (with $(b-1)\mid c$). Then
$$f(y+f(x))-f(y)=(b-1)f(x)=(b-1)^2x+c,$$
and
$$f(bx)-f(x)+c=(b-1)bx+\tfrac{c}{b-1}-(b-1)x-\tfrac{c}{b-1}+c=(b-1)^2x+c.$$
Both sides agree. ✓

---

**Answer.** A solution exists if and only if $(b-1)\mid c$, and in that case the unique solution is
$$\boxed{\,f(x)=(b-1)x+\dfrac{c}{\,b-1\,}\,}.$$

